 ## Overview
-Binary Radiance Fields (BiRF) is a storage-efficient radiance field representation that constrains feature grid parameters to binary values (+1 or -1) to achieve compact storage without post-processing compression [S1]. Unlike methods that compress pre-trained models or use real-valued floating-point grids, BiRF performs binarization during training using a straight-through estimator, allowing it to maintain reconstruction quality with significantly reduced memory footprint [S1]. It builds on [Model Architecture and Encoding](13550-model-architecture-and-encoding.md) by replacing real-valued feature grids with binary ones and employing a 2D-3D hybrid multi-resolution architecture to mitigate hash collisions inherent in 3D grids [S1].
+Binary Radiance Fields (BiRF) is a storage-efficient radiance field representation that constrains feature grid parameters to binary values (+1 or -1) to drastically reduce memory footprint while maintaining reconstruction quality [S1]. Unlike post-processing compression methods that require additional optimization steps and are bounded by pre-trained model performance, BiRF performs binarization during training using a 2D-3D hybrid multi-resolution architecture [S1]. It builds on [Model Architecture and Encoding](13550-model-architecture-and-encoding.md) by replacing real-valued feature lookups with binary-encoded hash grids, enabling compact storage (e.g., < 0.5 MB) without sacrificing the ability to model complex volumetric scenes [S1].
 
